WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.771
A decision flow you can defend.

2
00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:05.827
Start from the evidence you have, not from the technique you want to try.

3
00:00:06.107 --> 00:00:12.357
The flow below is the order most practitioners recommend, and every arrow is a measurement, not an opinion.

4
00:00:12.637 --> 00:00:13.939
Write evals first.

5
00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:24.651
OpenAI’s fine-tuning guide says to invest in fine-tuning only after setting up evals, and you can’t tell whether any option helped without them (Studio 3).

6
00:00:24.931 --> 00:00:25.869
Prompt well.

7
00:00:26.149 --> 00:00:29.925
Clear instructions, examples, structured outputs, a better model.

8
00:00:30.205 --> 00:00:31.677
Many problems end here.

9
00:00:31.957 --> 00:00:33.835
Diagnose the remaining failures.

10
00:00:34.115 --> 00:00:36.589
Read them (error analysis, Studio 3).

11
00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:38.661
Are they missing or stale facts?

12
00:00:38.941 --> 00:00:41.437
Or wrong behavior despite having the facts?

13
00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:43.552
Missing facts: add retrieval.

14
00:00:43.832 --> 00:00:44.813
Measure again.

15
00:00:45.093 --> 00:00:52.389
Behavior that prompting can’t fix, or a cost and latency target the prompted model can’t meet: consider fine-tuning.

16
00:00:52.669 --> 00:01:00.605
Usually that means a small open model trained on your task, compared against your prompted baseline on the same frozen test set.

17
00:01:00.885 --> 00:01:02.123
Both: combine.

18
00:01:02.403 --> 00:01:06.477
A fine-tuned model can learn to use retrieved context in your format.

19
00:01:06.757 --> 00:01:10.555
Retrieval supplies the facts; fine-tuning supplies the behavior.

20
00:01:10.835 --> 00:01:13.117
Two exits matter as much as the arrows.

21
00:01:13.397 --> 00:01:18.795
If fine-tuning doesn’t beat the prompted baseline on your test set, you stop and ship the prompt.

22
00:01:19.075 --> 00:01:23.469
And if the gain is real but smaller than the maintenance cost, you also stop.
